      "name": "ProcessCharacterSelectionInitialization",
      "signature": "int ProcessCharacterSelectionInitialization(void)",
      "calling_convention": "unknown",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Initializes and processes character selection state for the game client.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Allocates 0x130 bytes of stack space for local variables\n2. Saves registers (EBP, ESI, EDI) for safe restoration\n3. Calls game state initialization routine (offset +0x4076)\n4. Validates initialization success; jumps to cleanup on failure\n5. Loads global character and UI state pointers\n6. Processes and validates character slot data\n7. Configures UI control handlers and event callbacks\n8. Sets up character selection display state\n9. Restores registers and returns success code\n\nParameters:\n- None (uses global state)\n\nReturns:\n- EAX: int - 1 if initialization successful, 0 on failure or cleanup\n\nReturn Stack:\n- RETN 4 (returns with cleanup of 4-byte argument on stack)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Handles multiple character slots (0-7+ based on assembly)\n- Validates expansion pack availability\n- Sets up event handlers for UI controls\n- Processes different character class/type configurations\n\nError Handling:\n- Returns 0 on any initialization failure\n- Cleans up partially initialized state before returning\n- Restores all modified registers on early exit\n\nStack Frame:\n- [ESP + 0x0]: Return address\n- [ESP - 0x130]: Local variable buffer (304 bytes)\n- [EBP - 0x4]: Saved EBP\n- [EBP - 0x8]: Saved ESI\n- [EBP - 0xC]: Saved EDI\n\nNotes:\n- Function uses __stdcall calling convention (RETN 4)\n- Extensive use of global data structures for game state\n- Calls multiple helper functions for character processing\n- Heavy interaction with UI control subsystem\n- Function boundary detection issues due to large code size\n\nKnown Limitations:\n- Decompiler encounters errors on this function\n- Variable detection incomplete in Ghidra due to function structure\n- Assembly-level analysis required for detailed behavior",
